{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080379,"nonce":"j7ZWNLvaFc9RKbTrbDwDz8Ye+SU=","title":"The three flap knobs' `guide` verdicts still coach an operator through a damper that no longer exists, because the tests pin the levels and the suggestions","message":"**What is wrong.** The `what` and `risks` copy on `flap_threshold`, `flap_window_s` and `flap_digest_interval_s` was corrected for the retirement and now says the truth plainly — *\"Retained, and it changes no delivery\"* (`web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:488`, `:543`, `:604`). The `guide` closures directly below them were not. They still compute the retired detector's arithmetic and return live-sounding verdicts about a damper, so the row's prose says the number decides nothing and the verdict under the input box tells the operator how to make it work.\n\n**The demonstration.** Four sentences, on screen, today. `:522` — *\"The damper can never engage — it is dead code that looks configured. Widen the window rather than lowering the threshold.\"* The damper is gone; the value is dead at every setting, not only above the ceiling, and the remedy offered changes nothing. `:578` — *\"The window cannot contain a single fire-resolve-fire cycle, so no threshold is reachable\"* — carrying a `suggest` the operator can click to \"fix\" it. `:628` — *\"The digest starts arriving after anyone cared\"* — about a flapping digest that `:604` says two lines up was deleted. `:641` — *\"at or above group_interval, so it is a real digest\"* — asserting the existence of the thing the `what` copy denies.\n\nThe `amRule` strings were given a hedge rather than a rewrite, and the hedge is the admission: *\"The arithmetic below is the retired detector's, kept because the live verdicts on this row still compute it; no value here changes what is delivered\"* (`:500`, `:556`, `:614`). \"The live verdicts on this row still compute it\" is the defect stated by the person who wrote the sentence.\n\n**Why it matters.** The tuning screen's whole claim is that a number comes with the reasoning that sizes it, and the `guide` is the part an operator acts on — it is the line with the clickable `suggest` next to it. An operator on `flap_window_s` reads \"Retained, and it changes no delivery\", then reads \"no threshold is reachable\", then is offered a button that widens the window. Every one of those is a step toward believing they have re-armed something. The `inert` level is the cruellest of the three, because it exists to mean \"this value is unreachable and the mechanism will never fire\" — and it now says that about a value that would be equally inert at every other setting.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:484-537` (`flap_threshold`: `amRule` at `:500`, `guide` at `:501-537`, the `inert` verdict at `:520-523`, the `tight` verdict at `:525-530`, the `ok` at `:532-534`), `:538-602` (`flap_window_s`: `amRule` at `:556`, `guide` at `:557-601`, `inert` at `:576-580`, `tight` at `:582-587`, `ok` at `:589-595`), `:603-645` (`flap_digest_interval_s`: `amRule` at `:614`, `guide` at `:615-644`, the two `tight` arms and the `ok` at `:637-643`) · the tests that pin them: `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.test.ts:587-716` (`flap_threshold` — `shapeOf` asserted as `[\"ok\",\"tight\",\"inert\"]` at `:593`, the ceiling identity at `:598-660`, the `suggest` pinned to `boundsOf(\"flap_threshold\").min` at `:691`), `:718-765` (`flap_window_s` — `shapeOf` at `:722`, and `:725-748` asserting the two knobs agree *exactly* on level for every `(group_interval, threshold)` pair, plus the shipped-2h derivation at `:750-760`), `:291-293` (`flap_digest_interval_s`).\n\n**Scope.** medium. The copy edit is small; the test move is not. `tuningCopy.test.ts:725` asserts the threshold and window guides return the *same level* on the same two numbers for four `group_interval` values and four thresholds, and `:750-760` derives the shipped 2 h from the shipped threshold through `observableCycleS`. Both are good tests of a real invariant that no longer has a mechanism behind it, so the change is not \"fix the strings\" — it is deciding what these three rows say when there is nothing to size, and then deleting or repointing the arithmetic the tests protect.\n\nThis ticket is the copy half only. Whether the three keys survive at all is a settings-contract decision tracked separately; if they are removed, this work is deleted rather than done, so sequence the two.\n\n**Done when.** No `guide` on any of the three rows returns a verdict phrased as though a value could arm, disarm or pace anything. Whatever the three return — a single `inert` verdict naming the retirement and pointing at the case-retention window W, or nothing at all — is consistent with the `what` and `risks` copy above it, so the row does not contradict itself in two adjacent paragraphs. No `suggest` is offered on a key that decides nothing, because a suggestion is an invitation to click. `tuningCopy.test.ts` asserts the new shape rather than the retired detector's arithmetic, and the \"these two knobs agree exactly\" invariant is either deleted with the mechanism it described or moved to whatever still has one.","files":null}]}